<article class="env-lemma" id="09KC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09KC">Lemma <span data-tag="09KC">22.9.2</span>.</a> Let $(A, \text{d})$ be a differential graded algebra. Let $\alpha : K \to L$ and $\beta : L \to M$ define an admissible short exact sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to K \to L \to M \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> of differential graded $A$-modules. Let $(K, L, M, \alpha , \beta , \delta )$ be the associated triangle. Then the triangles </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  (M[-1], K, L, \delta [-1], \alpha , \beta ) \quad \text{and}\quad (M[-1], K, C(\delta [-1]), \delta [-1], i, p)  \]
</div>
  <p> are isomorphic. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using a choice of splittings we write $L = K \oplus M$ and we identify $\alpha $ and $\beta $ with the natural inclusion and projection maps. By construction of $\delta $ we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  d_ B = \left( \begin{matrix}  d_ K 

&  \delta 

\\  0 

&  d_ M 

\end{matrix} \right)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       On the other hand the cone of $\delta [-1] : M[-1] \to K$ is given as $C(\delta [-1]) = K \oplus M$ with differential identical with the matrix above! Whence the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>